WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:09.840
This simple-looking blob is actually a swarm
of millions of magnetic nanoparticles.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:17.880
The swarm is controlled by a rapidly-oscillating
magnetic field.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:25.560
Modifying the magnetic field allows the ‘microswarm’
to move about.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:43.000
It can also change direction and shape, split
up, rejoin, and even move through a maze.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.180
The idea is to imitate the swarms found in
nature,

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.200
in animals like birds or fish.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.100
These groups of individuals together form
large shapes, but they're much more flexible

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:59.120
than a single large creature would be.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.520
The same can be said of the microswarm.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.640
It’s strong enough to manipulate small objects,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.260
but flexible enough to fit through narrow spaces.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.280
This technology may have medical applications
– perhaps delivering drugs around the body

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:22.740
or acting as a contrast agent for X-rays or
ultrasound scans.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.120
But although this microswarm is pretty good
at getting through a simple maze,

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.280
the inside of the human body is a much tougher challenge.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:38.140
Tricky obstacles like fast flowing blood cells
and highly salty solutions can complicate things.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.540
So a lot more work is needed before magnetic nanoparticles

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.900
can start swarming around inside your body.

